        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Wahhabism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, later the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>; Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, the two countries whose native population is predominantly Wahhabi, and, through Saudi funding, mosques, schools and scholarships across the Muslim world</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">began in the early 18th century; pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a> 1744; three Saudi States, the first ending with the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in 1818; Third Saudi State and Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>'s oil revenues from 1939; changes to Saudi religious policy from 2017</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> (1703–1792), the movement's founder; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>, the local leader of the pact; Hamad ibn Nasir ibn Mu'ammar, who set out its legal theory; Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasan and Abd al-Latif ibn Abd al-Rahman, heads of the religious estate; Abd al-Aziz ibn Baz and Muhammad ibn al-Uthaymeen, leading scholars of the 20th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ibn Abd al-Wahhab's teachings were the official creed of the three Saudi States for more than two centuries; the movement shaped Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>'s religious institutions and, with Saudi money, spread widely, while its critics call it a deviant sect and its followers reject the name</span></div>
        </div>
