    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Followers called themselves Muwahhidun, 'the Monotheists'; the name Wahhabi was probably first used by Ibn Abd al-Wahhab's critic and brother Sulayman, and present-day followers continue to reject it, often calling themselves Salafi.</li><li>In 1744 Ibn Abd al-Wahhab formed a pact with the local leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>, a politico-religious alliance with the Saudi monarchy that lasted for more than 250 years.</li><li>Its central teaching holds that devotion to any being other than God, including the veneration of saints at their tombs, is shirk, while its opponents accused it of Kharijite excess and of readiness to declare other Muslims unbelievers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Wahhabi scholars of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> in 1951, ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1962, accepted the education of girls in 1964 and permitted television in 1965, after vigorous debate.</p></aside>
    </section>

