    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1744</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> forms a pact with the local leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>, joining the movement to the Saudi family</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1773</span><span class="rf-text">Ibn Abd al-Wahhab retires, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abdulaziz turns to an expansionist policy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1818</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> is destroyed in the Wahhabi Wars</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Hasa</a> leads Abd al-Latif ibn Abd al-Rahman to write tracts against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, Shia and British</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</a> and his army conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1962</span><span class="rf-text">Saudi Wahhabi religious authorities allow the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">The Saudi clergy expel the scholar al-Albani after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> on women's faces</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2022</span><span class="rf-text">Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> first marks Founding Day on 22 February, dating the state to 1727 and not to the 1744 pact</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab.jpg" alt="Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:محمد_بن_عبد_الوهاب.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">أبو آسر, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wahhabism arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, the central Arabian region, where rituals honouring Muslim saints and pilgrimages to their tombs and shrines were widespread. Its founder, the Hanbali scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> (1703–1792), and his followers drew on the Hanbali scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyya</a> (1263–1328), who had advocated a return to the purity of the first three generations, the salaf, to rid Muslims of bid'a, or innovation. They regarded his works as core references in theology, although the movement also repudiated taqlid to legal authorities, Ibn Taymiyya and Ibn Qayyim among them.</p><p>The movement belonged to the revivalist trends of the 18th and 19th centuries, including the Mahdist movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, the Senussi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> movement of Uthman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Dan Fodio</a>, the Faraizi movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Sayyid Ahmed Barelvi's Mujahidin and the Padri movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. These sought reform through a direct return to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and blamed Muslim decline on the infiltration of local beliefs and practices. An earlier Ottoman movement, the Kadizadeli of the 17th century, likewise condemned practices it saw as bid'ah.</p><p>Socio-politically, the movement has been described as the first major Arab-led revolt against the Turkish, Persian and other foreign empires that had dominated the Islamic world since the Mongol invasions and the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the 13th century. Ibn Abd al-Wahhab's father is reported to have criticised his son when he began public preaching at Huraymila, but the sources do not state the nature of the disagreement.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first aim was tawhid, the oneness of God. All Muslims uphold it; according to David Commins and others, the disagreement between Wahhabis and their opponents is over what constitutes it. Ibn Abd al-Wahhab held that correct worship was the test of belief and that any act or statement showing devotion to a being other than God was shirk, tantamount to idolatry. He included in such acts popular practices that made holy men intercessors with God, and in his Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhid">Tawhid</a> he denounced the veneration of trees and rocks. He accepted that Muhammad would intercede for believers, but only by God's permission. David Commins calls it the pivotal idea that Muslims who disagreed with his definition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> stood outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> altogether.</p><p>On takfir, Ibn Abd al-Wahhab wrote that he declared unbelievers only those who knew the religion of the Messenger and then insulted it, forbade people from it or showed enmity towards its practitioners. The movement rejected taqlid, imitation of past legal opinion, and upheld <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>, independent reasoning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>. Its adherents also urged al-wala' wa al-bara', loyalty and disassociation, which Ibn Abd al-Wahhab argued meant Muslims should not befriend or imitate non-Muslims or heretical Muslims.</p><p>He defined armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> as requiring a valid religious justification and declaration by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>, and described the campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> as defensive. He taught that a Muslim ruler is owed allegiance so long as he leads according to the Shari'ah, and that counsel to rulers should be private.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-saud.jpg" alt="Ibn Saud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_King_Abdulaziz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudi Press Agency, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1744 Ibn Abd al-Wahhab formed a pact with a local leader, Muhammad bin Saud, offering political obedience and promising that protection and propagation of the movement meant 'power and glory' and rule of 'lands and men'. The state-building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> led to military conflicts with rival tribal chiefs; Ibn Abd al-Wahhab advised the emir on tactics, urging a largely defensive strategy and adherence to Islamic rules of warfare. After his retirement in 1773, the emir Abdulaziz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>'s son and successor, launched offensive campaigns. Abdullah ibn Muhammad Aal Ash-Shaykh condemned military excesses during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-mecca">conquest of Mecca</a>, saying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> would not be taken captive and that killing women and children was not agreed to. The Emirate of Diriyah was destroyed in 1818, at the end of the Wahhabi Wars.</p><p>In 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Hasa</a>, a majority-Shia area, with British help; from then Abd al-Latif ibn Abd al-Rahman wrote tracts condemning Ottomans, Shia and British as polytheists and forbade Muslims to travel or stay in their lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in October 1924, and after the discovery of petroleum in 1939 oil revenues funded the spread of Wahhabi ideals. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, Ibn Saud imposed Wahhabi doctrines in a progressively gentler form.</p><p>Debate led Saudi authorities to allow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> in 1951, the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1962, girls' education in 1964 and television in 1965. In 1963 the clergy expelled the scholar al-Albani after he issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> permitting women to uncover their faces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-rida.jpg" alt="Rashid Rida" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuhammadRashidRida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> (1703–1792) was the founder and eponym. His son Abd Allah (1752–1826) headed the movement after his father retired in 1773 and, after the fall of the first Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. His grandson Sulayman ibn Abd Allah (1780–1818) wrote an influential treatise restricting travel to and residence in the land of idolaters. His brother Sulayman ibn Abd al-Wahhab, an ardent critic, probably first used the name Wahhabi. Fatimah, his daughter, was a scholar who travelled widely and taught men and women. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a> was the local leader of the 1744 pact.</p><p>The legal jurist Hamad ibn Nasir ibn Mu'ammar (1747–1810), a son-in-law and pupil of Ibn Abd al-Wahhab, set out the movement's legal theory in a treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a> and taqlid. Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasan led the religious estate in the second Saudi emirate, and his son Abd al-Latif ibn Abd al-Rahman (1810–1876) headed it in the 1860s and early 1870s. Ahmad al-Hifzi, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a>, led the movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>. Ghaliyya al-Wahhabiyya, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> woman from Turubah, rose to the rank of Amira al-Umara and led Wahhabi forces defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the Ottoman–Wahhabi war.</p><p>In the 20th century Muhammad ibn Ibrahim Al ash-Sheikh (1893–1969), Grand Mufti from 1953, was said to have dominated the religious estate. Abd al-Aziz ibn Baz (1910–1999) has been called the most prominent proponent of the movement in his time, and Muhammad ibn al-Uthaymeen (1925–2001) another giant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Muhammad Rashid Rida</a>, a Salafiyya reformer, worked to rehabilitate the Wahhabis in the Islamic world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The pact of 1744 established a politico-religious alliance with the Saudi monarchy that lasted for more than 250 years, and for more than two centuries Ibn Abd al-Wahhab's teachings were championed as the official creed in the three Saudi States. After the unification of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> the movement consolidated its rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, and Saudi oil revenues spent on books, schools, universities, mosques and scholarships gave Wahhabi ideals a 'preeminent position of strength' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> around the world.</p><p>Estimates of Saudi spending abroad include upward of $100 billion and $2–3 billion a year since 1975; Peter Mandaville calls such figures unreliable. Khaled Abou El Fadl names four causes of the movement's reach: Arab nationalism's welcome of a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, the call for a return to the salaf through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>, control of Mecca and Medina, and the oil industry.</p><p>At home the religious establishment's position has changed. Changes to Saudi religious policy by Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman">Mohammed bin Salman</a> since 2017 have led to crackdowns on Islamists, and by 2021 the waning power of the clerics and a nationalist narrative led to what has been described as a 'post-Wahhabi era'. Saudi Arabia's Founding Day, commemorated since 2022 on 22 February, marks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>'s ascension in 1727 and de-emphasises the 1744 pact, which has led to an official uncoupling of the clergy from the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal.jpg" alt="Muhammad Iqbal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allama_Iqbal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Iqbal Academy Pakistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The name remains contested. Followers call themselves Muwahhidun or, now, Salafi, and the name is mainly an exonym that its opponents used. King Salman has called the term a doctrine that does not exist in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Scholars differ on how it relates to Salafism: Ahmad Moussalli says all Wahhabis are Salafists but not all Salafists are Wahhabis. The Syrian-Albanian scholar al-Albani called Ibn Abd al-Wahhab a Salafi in creed but not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">fiqh</a>, and the Saudi clergy expelled him in 1963.</p><p>Critics among other Muslims have called the movement a deviant sect. The Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> scholar Ibn Abidin declared it a modern manifestation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> clerics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-azhar">Al-Azhar</a> called it a satanic faith. Supporters include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a>, who called it the first throb of life in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> while noting that its vision of the past was uncritical, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taha-hussein">Taha Hussein</a>, who credited it with awakening the Arab soul.</p><p>On militancy, the claims are disputed. A study commissioned by the European Parliament in 2013, by Claude Moniquet, named Wahhabism the main source of global terrorism; others hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> and Islamic State are not Wahhabi, and that Saudi senior scholars condemned suicide bombing. Scholars also note that post-Soviet governments and others use the label for any Islamic assertion or opposition.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Wahhabism is a Salafi revivalist movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> in the early 18th century around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> and, from the pact of 1744, was bound to the Saudi family. Its followers reject the name; its critics call it a deviant sect, and its influence spread widely with Saudi funding.</em></p>

